Kibana: Kibana is the user interface.

Without Kibana:

curl Elasticsearch API

With Kibana:

Search logs
Build dashboards
Filter by namespace
Filter by pod
Filter by container
Create visualizations
Explore logs interactively


Flow:

Java App

↓

stdout

↓

Container Runtime

↓

Node Log Files

↓

Fluent Bit

↓

Elasticsearch

↓

Kibana




Real Kubernetes Example

Your deployment:
    javawebapp
    Replica = 3

Pods:
    javawebapp-abc
    javawebapp-def
    javawebapp-ghi

Each pod produces:

User Login
Payment Success
Product Created

Fluent Bit collects from all three pods.
Elasticsearch stores all logs.

Kibana lets you search:
 namespace=default
 pod=javawebapp-abc
 level=ERROR


Kubernetes Metadata:
    One of Fluent Bit's biggest advantages is adding Kubernetes context.

Without metadata:
{
 "message":"Login Successful"
}

With metadata:
{
 "namespace":"default",
 "pod":"javawebapp-7d7fc",
 "container":"java",
 "node":"minikube",
 "message":"Login Successful"
}

Now you can answer questions like:

    Which pod generated this error?
    Which namespace?
    Which node?
    Which container?


Where Logs Are Stored

Your Java application does not write directly to Elasticsearch.

Flow:

Application
↓
stdout
↓
Container Runtime
↓
Node Files
↓
Fluent Bit
↓
Elasticsearch


Log-lifecycle:
Application generates log
↓
Fluent Bit detects new log
↓
Parses it
↓
Adds metadata
↓
Sends to Elasticsearch
↓
Elasticsearch indexes it
↓
Kibana displays it
